From 2c6e70fc9aa4456f4da0bc2320431d3ef24e7ad7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Saverio Moscardini <4923627+savemosca@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:57:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Self-heal bridge: stop an IRQ continuation at its own iret

runtime_irq() is entered from the interrupted code's runtime_tick and
returns into it, so the interrupted instruction resumes natively. When
the IRQ return itself is bridged, the bridge (IRQ-continuation mode)
executed the interpreted `subs pc, lr, #4` and kept interpreting the
interrupted code until the next static entry, and the native code then
ran those instructions again.

For cartridge code the next static entry is the interrupted PC (interior
dispatch entries), so nothing went wrong. The BIOS table lists function
entries only: with a BIOS corpus that lacks the IRQ wrapper tail at 0x138
(generated with `gba_recompile --bios` and no --config), an IRQ taken at
0x164 inside the SWI handler ran `push {r2, lr}`, `add lr, pc, #0` and
`bx r12` in the bridge and again natively. The System stack lost 8 bytes
and the caller's LR became 0x170; a later return entered Thumb code in
ARM state or reached the reset vector, and the bridge aborted on an
Undefined instruction. IRQs inside CpuSet/CpuFastSet/LZ77 loops finished
the routine in the bridge and then repeated its tail natively, writing
wrong data to VRAM. Seen in MOTHER 3 (EN) on Android, confirmed with the
runtime trace; with the fix the abort and the glitches are gone.

Stop the continuation as soon as the interpreted exception return from
IRQ mode matches this IRQ's nesting depth. Resumable contexts are left
as they were: there runtime_irq() returns at once and the dispatcher
resumes by PC, so no native frame is waiting.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp | 20 ++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp b/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp
index 4b16bf4..06a70e5 100644
--- a/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp
+++ b/src/runtime/runtime_arm_default_aborts.cpp
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 // and supply their own versions that record state for the diff runner.
 
 #include "runtime_arm.h"
+#include "runtime_context.h"
 #include "mod_function_hooks.h"
 #include "symbol_lookup.h"
 #include "self_heal.h"
@@ -423,6 +424,7 @@ extern "C" const char* g_tick_ctx;
 // runtime_irq() (g_call_return_floor): the floor keeps the cancel cascade from
 // corrupting mainline frames; this keeps the self-heal bridge from bailing early.
 extern "C" uint32_t g_irq_nest_depth;
+extern "C" uint32_t g_irq_iret_depth;
 
 // runtime_bridge_interpret — the interpret-the-missed-subtree core, factored out
 // of runtime_dispatch_miss so the P6 sljit differential gate can reuse it as its
@@ -624,6 +626,24 @@ extern "C" int runtime_bridge_interpret(uint32_t entry_pc, bool entry_thumb,
 
         if (!irq_cont && (cpu.R[15] & ~1u) == stop_pc) break;
 
+        // An IRQ continuation ends at its own iret. runtime_irq() was entered
+        // from the interrupted code's runtime_tick and returns into it, so that
+        // code resumes the interrupted instruction natively. Interpreting past
+        // the iret would execute those instructions a second time when it does.
+        // Heal-to-static below does not catch this when the interrupted code is
+        // the BIOS SWI handler: the BIOS table only lists function entries, so
+        // the bridge ran on from 0x164 through `push {r2, lr}` and `bx r12`,
+        // and the native handler then pushed again (System SP off by 8, LR
+        // 0x170 as the caller's LR: a later return lands in ARM state inside
+        // Thumb code, or at the reset vector).
+        // (A resumable context has no native frame waiting: runtime_irq()
+        // returned at once and the outer dispatcher resumes by PC.)
+        if (irq_cont && r == armv4t::Interpreter::Result::ExceptionReturn &&
+            old_mode == 0x12u && g_irq_iret_depth == g_irq_nest_depth &&
+            !gbarecomp::runtime_has_resumable_context()) {
+            break;
+        }
+
         // Heal-to-static: for a top-level miss (no reliable return address),
         // the moment the bridged subtree branches/returns back into a
         // statically-recompiled function, stop interpreting and let the main
